use std
into acoustic.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with ac_ is exported). It solves linear acoustics (the Helmholtz equation for the sound pressure) in an air or liquid region made of equal cubic cells on a grid: the steady sound field at one frequency with sound sources, non-reflecting (anechoic) ends, open ends and absorbing surfaces, and the natural acoustic modes of a cavity. It also builds a display surface of the cells. Its only import line is `import math;`, ending with a semicolon. Every int used in real math is converted with `as real`, and a real used as an int with `as int`, since Helium has no real or int conversion calls.

```amni
extern fn ac_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "ac_view_f64";
extern fn ac_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "ac_view_i32";
```

Cell layout. Level 0 holds the `NC` fluid cells that JavaScript lists; coarser levels 1 to `NL` minus 1 group the cells of the level below in blocks of 2 by 2 by 2. Every per-cell array holds all levels one after another: level l starts at offset LO[l] and has LC[l] cells, so cell c of level l is global entry LO[l] plus c. Each cell has 6 neighbor slots in `NB` (6 entries per cell, in the direction order minus x, plus x, minus y, plus y, minus z, plus z), holding the index of the neighbor cell within the same level or minus 1 when there is none, and 3 grid coordinates in `IJ` (i, j, k). Complex vectors are stored interleaved: entry 2c is the real part and entry 2c plus 1 the imaginary part of cell c. The operator of level l applied with the three reals ar, ai and bi and the level scale s equal to LS[l] has, for global cell g, the diagonal coefficient with real part s times LD[g] plus ar times MD[g] and imaginary part ai times MD[g] plus bi times BD[g], and toward the neighbor in direction d the coefficient with real part minus s times LW[6g plus d] plus ar times MW[6g plus d] and imaginary part ai times MW[6g plus d].

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `NC` (level 0 cell count), `NL` (level count). Reals, all starting at 0.0: `KA`, `KB`, `KC` (the ar, ai and bi of the operator being solved), `QA`, `QB`, `QC` (the ar, ai and bi of the preconditioner), `BT` (the last Lanczos beta). Growable f64 arrays: `LD`, `MD`, `BD` (1 per cell, all levels), `LW`, `MW` (6 per cell, all levels), `E`, `R`, `T` (2 per cell, all levels, complex), `LS` (1 per level), `WB`, `DR`, `SR`, `PW` (1 per level 0 cell: boundary admittance face count, open face count, source strength, port face count), `X`, `BB`, `RS`, `RH`, `PD`, `VV`, `YY`, `SS`, `ZZ`, `TT` (2 per level 0 cell, complex), `VAL` (1 per level 0 cell), `LV`, `WV`, `MV`, `MS`, `TA`, `TV`, `TW`, `DA`, `DB`, `OUT`, `PR` (8 parameters), `ST` (8 statistics), `SP`, `SW`, `SC`, `FR` (1 per grid cell: solid fraction), `GV`, `GF` (1 per boundary group). Growable i32 arrays: `GM`, `FL`, `CI` (1 per grid cell), `GK` (1 per boundary group), `STK`, `CS`, `CP`, `NB`, `IJ`, `CO` (1 per cell, all levels: the index of the block that holds it on the next level, minus 1 on the last level), `PT` (1 per level 0 cell: port index or minus 1), `LC`, `LO`, `GD` (3 per level: grid size along x, y and z), `MP`, `NM`, `SI`, `OD`.

Parameters in `PR`: 0 the dimensionless wavenumber kappa (wavenumber times cell size), 1 relative tolerance (default 1e-6), 2 iteration limit (2000), 3 Jacobi damping (0.7), 4 smoothing sweeps (2), 5 preconditioner shift (0.5), 6 mass blend gamma (0.0714285714285714), 7 coarsest cell count (64). Statistics in `ST`: 0 iterations of the last solve, 1 its final relative residual, 2 status (0 converged, 1 not converged), 3 level count, 4 coarsest level cell count, 5 largest `VAL`, 6 smallest `VAL`, 7 Lanczos steps taken, 8 fluid grid cells dropped as unconnected, 9 connected fluid pieces found.

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `zi(xs, n, x)` does the same for an i32 growable array, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. `zr(xs, n, cap, x)` and `zri(xs, n, cap, x)` do the same for an f64 and an i32 growable array but reserve cap entries (cap is at least n), so that later pushes up to cap entries fit. On the web an array that holds entries cannot grow past the room it first reserved, so every array gets all its room while it is empty: arrays are sized through these helpers, `ac_build` pushes only into the room `ac_setup` reserved, and `ac_cells` reserves its stacks before pushing.

The exported function `ac_views()`, declared with the export keyword, registers the arrays JavaScript reads or writes through ac_view_f64 and ac_view_i32: f64 views 1 `WB`, 2 `DR`, 3 `SR`, 4 `PW`, 5 `PR`, 6 `ST`, 7 `X`, 8 `OUT`, 9 `VAL`, 10 `MS`, 11 `SP`, 12 `SW`; i32 views 13 `NB`, 14 `IJ`, 15 `PT`, 16 `SI`; f64 views 17 `FR`, 18 `GV`, 19 `GF`; i32 views 20 `GM`, 21 `GK`. It returns 0.

The exported function `ac_setup(n, gx, gy, gz)`, declared with the export keyword, takes four i32 values: the level 0 cell count and the grid size along x, y and z. It sets `NC` to n and `NL` to 1, sizes with `zri` `NB` to 6n entries of minus 1 with room for 12n, `IJ` to 3n zeros with room for 6n and `CO` to n entries of minus 1 with room for 2n, sizes with `zr` `LD`, `MD` and `BD` to n zeros with room for 2n, `LW` and `MW` to 6n zeros with room for 12n and `E`, `R` and `T` to 2n zeros with room for 4n, sizes `PT` to n entries of minus 1, `WB`, `DR`, `SR`, `PW` and `VAL` to n zeros, `X`, `BB`, `RS`, `RH`, `PD`, `VV`, `YY`, `SS`, `ZZ` and `TT` to 2n zeros, `LC` to the single entry n, `LO` to the single entry 0, `GD` to the three entries gx, gy and gz, `LS` to the single entry 1.0, `OUT` to 1 zero, empties `MS`, `SP`, `SW`, `SC` and `SI`, sizes `ST` to 10 zeros and `PR` to the 8 values 0.0, 1e-6, 2000.0, 0.7, 2.0, 0.5, 0.0714285714285714, 64.0, then calls `ac_views` and returns n.

The exported function `ac_shape(n, gx, gy, gz)`, declared with the export keyword, takes four i32 values (a level 0 cell count and the grid size along x, y and z) and prepares only what `ac_skin` needs, so a display surface can be rebuilt from stored cell data without the solver arrays: it sets `NC` to n, sizes `NB` to 6n entries of minus 1, `IJ` to 3n zeros and `VAL` to n zeros, sets `GD` to the three entries gx, gy and gz, calls `ac_views` and returns n.

The exported function `ac_grid(gx, gy, gz, ng)`, declared with the export keyword, takes four i32 values: the grid size along x, y and z and the boundary group count. With nt equal to gx times gy times gz, it sizes `FR` to nt zeros, `GM` to nt entries of minus 1, `GK` to ng zeros, `GV` and `GF` to ng zeros, calls `ac_views` and returns nt.

The exported function `ac_cells(gx, gy, gz, region, ng)`, declared with the export keyword, takes five i32 values (the grid size along x, y and z, the region kind and the boundary group count) and turns the grid that JavaScript filled through `ac_grid` into the level 0 cells. Grid cell (i, j, k) has index (k times gy plus j) times gx plus i, and its 6 grid neighbors follow the direction order minus x, plus x, minus y, plus y, minus z, plus z; a neighbor outside the grid does not exist. `FR` holds the solid fraction of each grid cell, `GM` the boundary group that owns a grid cell outside the fluid (or minus 1), `GK` the kind of each group (0 sound source, 1 anechoic end, 2 open end, 3 absorbing lining) and `GV` the value of each group (the source amplitude for a source, the admittance for a lining). nt is gx times gy times gz. `STK` is emptied and reserves nt entries, and `CS` and `CP` are emptied and each reserve nt entries. First `FL` is sized with `zi` to nt zeros and FL[c] gets 1 for every grid cell c with FR[c] at least 0.5. When region is 1 (the fluid fills the passages inside the solid), every grid cell on the outer layer of the grid (i equal to 0 or gx minus 1, j equal to 0 or gy minus 1, or k equal to 0 or gz minus 1) with FL equal to 0 and GM below 0 gets FL equal to 2 and is pushed on `STK`, and while `STK` is not empty a popped cell spreads FL equal to 2 to each of its grid neighbors with FL equal to 0 and GM below 0, pushing them; then every grid cell with FL equal to 0 and GM below 0 gets FL equal to 3. When region is 0 (the solid body is the fluid), every grid cell with FL equal to 1 gets FL equal to 3. FL equal to 3 marks fluid. Next the connected fluid pieces: `CI` is sized with `zi` to nt entries of minus 1; for each grid cell c with FL[c] equal to 3 and CI[c] below 0, a new piece number p (the length of `CS`) starts, `CS` gets pushed 0 and `CP` gets pushed 0, CI[c] gets p and c is pushed on the emptied `STK`; while `STK` is not empty a popped cell q adds 1 to CS[p] and, for each grid neighbor r of q, when FL[r] is 3 and CI[r] is below 0, CI[r] gets p and r is pushed, and when FL[r] is not 3, GM[r] is at least 0 and GK[GM[r]] is at most 2, CP[p] gets 1. Then when some piece has CP equal to 1 every piece with CP equal to 1 is kept, and otherwise only the first largest piece by `CS` is kept; every fluid grid cell of a piece that is not kept gets FL equal to 4, and drop counts those cells. Then the kept fluid grid cells are numbered in grid order: n starts at 0, and for each grid cell c, CI[c] gets n and n grows by 1 when FL[c] is 3, and CI[c] gets minus 1 otherwise. It calls ac_setup(n, gx, gy, gz), sizes `GF` with `zf` to ng zeros, and for each grid cell c with FL[c] equal to 3 and m equal to CI[c], IJ[3m], IJ[3m plus 1] and IJ[3m plus 2] get its i, j and k, and for each direction d from 0 to 5 with grid neighbor r: when r exists and FL[r] is 3, NB[6m plus d] gets CI[r]; otherwise g is GM[r] when r exists and minus 1 when it does not, and when g is at least 0 the face belongs to group g: GF[g] grows by 1.0; kind GK[g] equal to 0 adds 1.0 to WB[m] and GV[g] to SR[m]; kind 1 adds 1.0 to WB[m]; kind 2 adds 1.0 to DR[m]; kind 3 adds GV[g] to WB[m]; and for kinds 0, 1 and 2, PT[m] gets g when it is below 0, and then PW[m] grows by 1.0 when PT[m] equals g. Finally ST[8] gets drop as real and ST[9] the length of `CS` as real; it calls `ac_views` and returns n.

The exported function `ac_build()`, declared with the export keyword, runs after JavaScript filled `NB`, `IJ`, `WB` and `DR` for level 0, and builds the operators of every level. gamma is PR[6]. First level 0: for each cell c below `NC`, nf is the number of directions d (0 to 5) with NB[6c plus d] at least 0; LD[c] is nf as real plus 2.0 times DR[c]; MD[c] is 1.0 minus 6.0 times gamma plus gamma times (6 minus nf) as real; BD[c] is WB[c]; for each d, LW[6c plus d] is 1.0 and MW[6c plus d] is gamma when NB[6c plus d] is at least 0, and both are 0.0 otherwise. Then it adds coarser levels while `NL` is below 14: l is `NL` minus 1, nl is LC[l] and o is LO[l]; it stops when nl as real is at most PR[7], or when LO[l] plus 2 times nl is above 2 times `NC` (the room `ac_setup` reserved). The coarse grid size is cx equal to (GD[3l] plus 1) div 2, cy equal to (GD[3l plus 1] plus 1) div 2 and cz equal to (GD[3l plus 2] plus 1) div 2; `MP` is sized with `zi` to cx times cy times cz entries of minus 1; oc is o plus nl and cnt starts at 0. First pass, for each c from 0 below nl with g equal to o plus c and i, j, k equal to IJ[3g], IJ[3g plus 1], IJ[3g plus 2]: q is ((k div 2) times cy plus j div 2) times cx plus i div 2; when MP[q] is below 0, MP[q] gets cnt, `IJ` gets pushed i div 2, j div 2 and k div 2, `NB` gets pushed 6 entries of minus 1, `LD`, `MD` and `BD` each get pushed 0.0, `LW` and `MW` each get pushed 6 zeros, `CO` gets pushed minus 1, `E`, `R` and `T` each get pushed 2 zeros, and cnt grows by 1; then CO[g] gets MP[q]. Second pass, for each c from 0 below nl with g equal to o plus c, cc equal to CO[g] and G equal to oc plus cc: LD[G] grows by LD[g], MD[G] by MD[g] and BD[G] by BD[g]; for each d from 0 to 5 with nb equal to NB[6g plus d] at least 0, nn is CO[o plus nb]; when nn equals cc, LD[G] drops by LW[6g plus d] and MD[G] grows by MW[6g plus d]; otherwise LW[6G plus d] grows by LW[6g plus d], MW[6G plus d] grows by MW[6g plus d] and NB[6G plus d] gets nn. Then `LC` gets pushed cnt, `LO` gets pushed oc, `GD` gets pushed cx, cy and cz, `LS` gets pushed LS[l] times 0.5, and `NL` grows by 1; when cnt equals nl the loop stops. After the loop ST[3] is `NL` as real and ST[4] is LC[`NL` minus 1] as real; it calls `ac_views` and returns `NL`.

`cop(l, ar, ai, bi, xs, xo, ys, yo)` takes an i32 level l, three reals, an f64 growable array xs read by reference, an i32 offset xo, an f64 growable array ys written by reference and an i32 offset yo; xs and ys are always different arrays. It applies the operator of level l with ar, ai and bi (see the cell layout) to the complex vector whose cell c sits at entries 2 times (xo plus c) and 2 times (xo plus c) plus 1 of xs, and writes the result for cell c to entries 2 times (yo plus c) and 2 times (yo plus c) plus 1 of ys, for every c from 0 below LC[l]. With s equal to LS[l] and g equal to LO[l] plus c: the diagonal coefficient (dr, di) times (xr, xi) of cell c, plus for each direction d with nb equal to NB[6g plus d] at least 0 the neighbor coefficient (or, oi) times the complex value of cell nb (at entries 2 times (xo plus nb) and that plus 1), where a complex product (a, b) times (p, q) is (a p minus b q, a q plus b p). It returns 0.

`jac(l)` takes an i32 level and does one damped Jacobi sweep on level l with the preconditioner reals `QA`, `QB` and `QC`: it calls cop(l, `QA`, `QB`, `QC`, `E`, LO[l], `T`, LO[l]); then for each cell c below LC[l] with g equal to LO[l] plus c, s equal to LS[l], the diagonal coefficient dr equal to s times LD[g] plus `QA` times MD[g] and di equal to `QB` times MD[g] plus `QC` times BD[g], and the complex residual (rr, ri) equal to R minus T at entries 2g and 2g plus 1, it adds PR[3] times the complex quotient (rr, ri) divided by (dr, di) to E at entries 2g and 2g plus 1, where (a, b) divided by (p, q) is ((a p plus b q) divided by (p squared plus q squared), (b p minus a q) divided by (p squared plus q squared)). It returns 0.

`dense(l)` takes an i32 level and solves the preconditioner equations of level l exactly: m is LC[l] and o is LO[l]. `DA` is sized with `zf` to 2 times m times m zeros (complex, row r column q at entries 2 times (r times m plus q) and that plus 1) and `DB` to 2m zeros; `OD` is sized with `zi` to m zeros. For each row r below m with g equal to o plus r and s equal to LS[l]: the diagonal entry is (s times LD[g] plus `QA` times MD[g], `QB` times MD[g] plus `QC` times BD[g]); for each d with nb equal to NB[6g plus d] at least 0, entry (r, nb) grows by (minus s times LW[6g plus d] plus `QA` times MW[6g plus d], `QB` times MW[6g plus d]); DB at 2r and 2r plus 1 gets R at 2g and 2g plus 1. Then Gaussian elimination with partial pivoting: for each column q below m it picks the row p from q up to m minus 1 whose entry (p, q) has the largest squared modulus, swaps rows p and q of `DA` and entries p and q of `DB` (both complex), and when that pivot modulus is above 1e-300 it eliminates column q from every row below q using complex products and quotients. Then back substitution from the last row up, giving the complex solution x[r] that it writes to E at entries 2 times (o plus r) and that plus 1; a row whose pivot is 1e-300 or less gets x zero. It returns 0.

`vcy(l)` takes an i32 level and runs one multigrid V-cycle on level l, solving the preconditioner equations with right side `R` into `E` for that level: it first zeroes the entries of `E` for the cells of level l. When l equals `NL` minus 1 it calls dense(l) if LC[l] is at most 400, and otherwise calls jac(l) 30 times. Otherwise, with ns equal to PR[4] as int: it calls jac(l) ns times; calls cop(l, `QA`, `QB`, `QC`, `E`, LO[l], `T`, LO[l]); zeroes `R` for the cells of level l plus 1; for each cell c of level l with g equal to LO[l] plus c and cg equal to LO[l plus 1] plus CO[g], R at 2cg and 2cg plus 1 grows by R minus T at 2g and 2g plus 1; calls vcy(l plus 1); then for each cell c of level l, E at 2g and 2g plus 1 grows by E at 2cg and 2cg plus 1; and calls jac(l) ns times again. It returns 0.

`prec(rs, zs)` takes two f64 growable arrays by reference, rs read and zs written, always different arrays: it copies the first 2 times `NC` entries of rs into `R`, calls vcy(0), and copies the first 2 times `NC` entries of `E` into zs. It returns 0.

`amul(xs, ys)` takes two f64 growable arrays by reference, xs read and ys written, always different arrays, and returns cop(0, `KA`, `KB`, `KC`, xs, 0, ys, 0).

`cdr(xs, ys)` takes two f64 growable arrays by reference (read only, possibly the same array) and returns the sum over c below `NC` of xs[2c] times ys[2c] plus xs[2c plus 1] times ys[2c plus 1] (the real part of the conjugate of x times y). `cdi(xs, ys)` returns the sum of xs[2c] times ys[2c plus 1] minus xs[2c plus 1] times ys[2c] (the imaginary part).

`bicg()` solves, with BiCGStab preconditioned on the right by `prec`, the complex equations with the operator of level 0 and the reals `KA`, `KB` and `KC` (applied by `amul`), right side `BB`, starting from the current `X`. tol is PR[1] and itmax is PR[2] as int. bn is the square root of cdr(`BB`, `BB`); when bn is 0.0 it zeroes `X`, sets ST[0], ST[1] and ST[2] to 0.0 and returns 0. Otherwise: amul(`X`, `TT`); `RS` is `BB` minus `TT`; `RH` is a copy of `RS`; `VV` and `PD` are zeroed; the complex scalars rho, alpha and omega start at (1, 0). For it from 1 up to itmax: rhon is (cdr(`RH`, `RS`), cdi(`RH`, `RS`)); when its modulus is 0.0 the loop ends; beta is (rhon divided by rho) times (alpha divided by omega); `PD` becomes `RS` plus beta times (`PD` minus omega times `VV`); prec(`PD`, `YY`); amul(`YY`, `VV`); alpha is rhon divided by (cdr(`RH`, `VV`), cdi(`RH`, `VV`)); `SS` is `RS` minus alpha times `VV`; when the square root of cdr(`SS`, `SS`) is at most tol times bn, `X` grows by alpha times `YY`, ST[0] gets it as real, ST[1] that norm divided by bn, ST[2] 0.0, and it returns it; otherwise prec(`SS`, `ZZ`); amul(`ZZ`, `TT`); omega is (cdr(`TT`, `SS`), cdi(`TT`, `SS`)) divided by cdr(`TT`, `TT`) (a real); `X` grows by alpha times `YY` plus omega times `ZZ`; `RS` becomes `SS` minus omega times `TT`; rho becomes rhon; rn is the square root of cdr(`RS`, `RS`); ST[0] gets it as real and ST[1] gets rn divided by bn; when rn is at most tol times bn, ST[2] gets 0.0 and it returns it. All products of a complex scalar and a vector entry pair are complex products. When the loop ends without meeting the tolerance ST[2] gets 1.0 and it returns ST[0] as int.

The exported function `ac_solve(kap)`, declared with the export keyword, takes a real kappa (wavenumber times cell size) and solves the sound field at that frequency: PR[0] gets kap; `KA` is minus kap squared, `KB` is 0.0 and `KC` is kap; `QA` is minus kap squared, `QB` is PR[5] times kap squared and `QC` is kap; for each cell c below `NC`, BB[2c] is 0.0 and BB[2c plus 1] is 2.0 times kap times SR[c]; then it returns bicg(). `X` keeps the result (the complex sound pressure amplitude in pascals per cell) and is the starting guess of the next call.

The exported function `ac_zero()`, declared with the export keyword, zeroes `X` and returns 0.

The exported function `ac_ports(np)`, declared with the export keyword, takes an i32 port count: `OUT` is sized with `zf` to 5 times np zeros; for each cell c below `NC` with k equal to PT[c] at least 0 and below np, with w equal to PW[c], pr equal to X[2c], pi equal to X[2c plus 1] and a2 equal to pr squared plus pi squared: OUT[5k] grows by w, OUT[5k plus 1] by w times a2, OUT[5k plus 2] by w times pr, OUT[5k plus 3] by w times pi, and OUT[5k plus 4] becomes the larger of itself and the square root of a2. It calls `ac_views` and returns np.

The exported function `ac_field(kind)`, declared with the export keyword, takes an i32 kind and fills `VAL` for every cell c below `NC`, with a equal to the square root of X[2c] squared plus X[2c plus 1] squared: kind 0 gives the sound pressure level in decibels, 20.0 times the base 10 logarithm of the larger of a and 1e-12 divided by 2.8284271247461903e-5; kind 1 gives a; kind 2 gives X[2c]; a kind of 10 or more gives the absolute value of MS[(kind minus 10) times `NC` plus c] (a mode shape), and then every entry is divided by the largest of them when that is above 0.0. ST[5] gets the largest and ST[6] the smallest entry of `VAL`. It returns `NC`.

`mmul(xs, xo, ys, yo)` takes an f64 growable array xs read by reference, an i32 offset xo, an f64 growable array ys written by reference and an i32 offset yo, always different arrays, and applies the level 0 mass operator to a real vector: for each c below `NC`, ys[yo plus c] is MD[c] times xs[xo plus c] plus, for each d with nb equal to NB[6c plus d] at least 0, MW[6c plus d] times xs[xo plus nb]. It returns 0.

`jeig(k)` takes an i32 size and finds all eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the symmetric k by k matrix in `TA` (row-major) by cyclic Jacobi rotations: `TV` is sized with `zf` to k times k zeros with 1.0 on the diagonal; nf is the sum of the squares of all `TA` entries; for up to 100 sweeps, off is the sum of the squares of the entries above the diagonal, and the sweeps stop when off is at most 1e-30 times nf; in a sweep, for each p below k and q from p plus 1 below k with apq equal to TA[p times k plus q] not 0.0: th is (TA[q times k plus q] minus TA[p times k plus p]) divided by 2.0 times apq, t is 1.0 when th is at least 0.0 and minus 1.0 otherwise, divided by the absolute value of th plus the square root of th squared plus 1.0, cs is 1.0 divided by the square root of t squared plus 1.0 and sn is t times cs; then for each r below k, with x equal to TA[r times k plus p] and y equal to TA[r times k plus q], TA[r times k plus p] becomes cs x minus sn y and TA[r times k plus q] becomes sn x plus cs y; then for each r below k, with x equal to TA[p times k plus r] and y equal to TA[q times k plus r], TA[p times k plus r] becomes cs x minus sn y and TA[q times k plus r] becomes sn x plus cs y; then the same column update as the first one on `TV`. Finally `TW` is sized with `zf` to k zeros and TW[i] gets TA[i times k plus i]. Column e of `TV` (entries TV[r times k plus e]) is the eigenvector of TW[e]. It returns 0.

The exported function `ac_modes(nev, m, tau)`, declared with the export keyword, takes two i32 counts (modes wanted and Lanczos steps) and a real shift tau above 0.0, and finds the lowest natural modes of the level 0 equations (stiffness L plus open faces, mass M) by shift-invert Lanczos with the solver of `bicg`: n is `NC`; `KA` and `QA` get tau and `KB`, `KC`, `QB` and `QC` get 0.0; `LV` is sized with `zf` to (m plus 1) times n zeros, `WV` and `MV` to n zeros, and `TA` to m times m zeros (row-major with row length m). The start vector LV[c] is 1.0 plus 0.5 times the sine of 1.7 times c as real plus 0.3; mmul(`LV`, 0, `MV`, 0), then every LV[c] is divided by the square root of the sum of LV[c] times MV[c]. steps starts at 0. For j from 0 below m: mmul(`LV`, j times n, `MV`, 0); `BB` gets MV[c] at 2c and 0.0 at 2c plus 1; `X` is zeroed; bicg(); WV[c] gets X[2c]; mmul(`WV`, 0, `MV`, 0); alpha is the sum of LV[j times n plus c] times MV[c]; TA[j times m plus j] gets alpha; WV drops by alpha times the vector j of `LV`, and when j is above 0 also by TA[(j minus 1) times m plus j] times the vector j minus 1 of `LV`; then two times: mmul(`WV`, 0, `MV`, 0), and for each i from 0 up to j, ci is the sum of LV[i times n plus c] times MV[c], and WV drops by ci times the vector i of `LV`. Then mmul(`WV`, 0, `MV`, 0), beta is the square root of the larger of 0.0 and the sum of WV[c] times MV[c], `BT` gets beta and steps gets j plus 1; when beta is at most 1e-12 times the absolute value of alpha plus 1e-300, or j equals m minus 1, the loop ends; otherwise TA[j times m plus j plus 1] and TA[(j plus 1) times m plus j] get beta and the vector j plus 1 of `LV` gets WV divided by beta. Then k is steps; the leading k by k block of `TA` is copied into a fresh row-major k by k `TA` (row length k), and jeig(k) runs. The order of eigenvalues is by `TW` from largest to smallest. nm is the smaller of nev and k; `OUT` is sized with `zf` to 2 times nm zeros and `MS` to nm times n zeros. For each q below nm with e the index of the q-th largest `TW` and th equal to TW[e]: OUT[2q] is 1.0 divided by th minus tau when th is above 0.0 and 1e30 otherwise, OUT[2q plus 1] is the absolute value of `BT` times TV[(k minus 1) times k plus e] divided by the larger of the absolute value of th and 1e-300; MS[q times n plus c] is the sum over j below k of TV[j times k plus e] times LV[j times n plus c]. ST[7] gets steps as real; it calls `ac_views` and returns nm.

The exported function `ac_skin(axis, cut, side)`, declared with the export keyword, takes three i32 values and builds a display surface of the level 0 cells as triangles over the grid nodes, counting first and filling second so that every array is sized while it is empty. gx, gy and gz are GD[0], GD[1] and GD[2], and a grid node (x, y, z) has key (z times (gy plus 1) plus y) times (gx plus 1) plus x. A cell c is shown when cut is below 0, or side is 0 and IJ[3c plus axis] is at most cut, or side is not 0 and IJ[3c plus axis] is at least cut. A face of a shown cell c in direction d from 0 to 5, with nb equal to NB[6c plus d], is drawn when nb is below 0 or cell nb is not shown; for it, ax is d div 2, up is d mod 2, u is (ax plus 1) mod 3 and v is (ax plus 2) mod 3, the base node b is the cell coordinates (IJ[3c], IJ[3c plus 1], IJ[3c plus 2]) with up added on axis ax, corner 0 is b, corner 1 is b plus 1 on axis u, corner 2 is b plus 1 on axes u and v, and corner 3 is b plus 1 on axis v. First pass: `NM` is sized with `zi` to (gx plus 1) times (gy plus 1) times (gz plus 1) entries of minus 1, nf and nv start at 0, and for each drawn face nf grows by 1 and each corner whose NM[key] is below 0 gets NM[key] equal to nv and nv grows by 1. Then `SP` is sized with `zf` to 3 times nv zeros, `SW` and `SC` to nv zeros, and `SI` with `zi` to 6 times nf zeros; for each key with q equal to NM[key] at least 0, SP[3q], SP[3q plus 1] and SP[3q plus 2] get key mod (gx plus 1), (key div (gx plus 1)) mod (gy plus 1) and key div ((gx plus 1) times (gy plus 1)) as reals. Second pass over the same drawn faces in the same order, with t starting at 0: each corner vertex NM[key] gets SW plus VAL[c] and SC plus 1.0, and SI[6t] to SI[6t plus 5] get the triangles (corner 0, corner 1, corner 2) and (corner 0, corner 2, corner 3) when up is 1 and (corner 0, corner 2, corner 1) and (corner 0, corner 3, corner 2) when up is 0, as the vertex numbers NM[key] of those corners, then t grows by 1. Then each vertex with SC above 0.0 gets SW divided by SC. It calls `ac_views` and returns nf times 2.

`main()` does nothing.
